</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our locality-aware sampling strategy leverages the weighted reservoir sampling algorithm as its core mechanism.
Given an initial node set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{b}=\{v_{1},...v_{i},...\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{b}=\{v_{1},...v_{i},...\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph sampling procedure performs neighbor sampling on each node <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> according to predefined fanout settings.
In contrast to random sampling strategies, we adopt Algo. <a href="#alg2" title='Algorithm 2 ‣ III-A Locality-Aware Graph Sampling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to sample the neighborhood of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve a better cache hit rate.
Algo. <a href="#alg2" title='Algorithm 2 ‣ III-A Locality-Aware Graph Sampling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> samples a subset <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> vertices from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, prioritizing vertices based on their weights <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> which modulate sampling probability.
The algorithm iteratively compares the weight <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of each incoming vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to the minimum <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the replacement condition in line 7 is met, the vertex with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Algo. <a href="#alg2" title='Algorithm 2 ‣ III-A Locality-Aware Graph Sampling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> has a time complexity of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|\mathcal{N}(v_{i})|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|\mathcal{N}(v_{i})|)</annotation></semantics></math> and a space complexity of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, setting higher weight values for cached vertices enables fine-grained control over preferential sampling of cached data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Locality-aware sampling inherently prioritizes cached nodes.
Consequently, the batch generation phase may eliminate a significant number of duplicate nodes, substantially alleviating GPU memory pressure.
While this shrinking of subgraphs inevitably introduces some accuracy degradation, experimental evidence across various datasets suggests that the robust graph topology can reduce this impact.
The relationship among graph density <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, locality-aware sampling (governed by the bias rate <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature cache volume <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>), and accuracy drop <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A</annotation></semantics></math> can be modeled as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title='In III-A Locality-Aware Graph Sampling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A=f_{1}(\eta,\gamma,d(G),\Theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A=f_{1}(\eta,\gamma,d(G),\Theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\frac{|Vs_{i}|}{|V|},Vs_{i}\in Gs_{i}(Vs_{i},Es_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\frac{|Vs_{i}|}{|V|},Vs_{i}\in Gs_{i}(Vs_{i},Es_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the overlapping ratio of subgraph for training and the entire graph.
Generally, increasing the bias rate <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> leads to greater accuracy drop; however, this effect is alleviated with growing cache volume <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, graph partition before training can also reduce accuracy, because <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN is then unable to access information from remote partitions without high-speed NVLink interconnects.
Based on this model, we can adaptively select sampling configurations.
In the worst case, setting the bias rate to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively reverts the locality-aware strategy to naive random sampling, resulting in the same accuracy as existing works.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, locality-aware sampling offers a trade-off with reduced memory footprint in exchange for some drop in test accuracy, thereby contributing to affordable and adaptive GNN training, which can be validated with further ablation study.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimizations targeting only the sampling stage offer limited gains beyond a certain point.
When sampling is no longer the training bottleneck, our focus should shift to optimizing the training stage, highlighting the need for efficient parallelization and pipelining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fixed parallelism scheduling has been widely used, yet different tasks benefit from different schemes.
For example, evaluations on Reddit dataset show that finer-grained parallelism increases throughput with more workers.
To address this, we designed an adaptive, multi-level parallelism scheduling.
It dynamically switches among pipeline strategies, balancing context switching overhead and throughput.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2511.07421v1/Pipeline.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/206;" width="457" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Parallelism scheduling templates of <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our multi-level parallelism has <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> modes, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title='Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The sequential strategy minimizes overhead in constrained resources.
It executes each stage serially, eliminating data contention and reducing memory overhead.
However, the sequential execution also limits overall throughput.
To address this limitation, parallel mode <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> integrates and parallelizes sampling and batch generation.
According to Amdahl’s Law, the throughput <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of parallel mode <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title='In III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), letting <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of worker processes on each GPU:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}=f_{2}(\max\{\frac{1}{n}(\mathcal{T}_{sample}+\mathcal{T}_{batch}),\mathcal{T}_{train}\}\cdot iter,d(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}=f_{2}(\max\{\frac{1}{n}(\mathcal{T}_{sample}+\mathcal{T}_{batch}),\mathcal{T}_{train}\}\cdot iter,d(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, mode <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> increases GPU memory from process duplication, and introduces resource contention.
Expected memory footprint <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be modeled as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title='In III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=n\cdot f_{3}({num}_{GPU}\cdot\Theta,B,|M|,Runtime,d(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=n\cdot f_{3}({num}_{GPU}\cdot\Theta,B,|M|,Runtime,d(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The peak GPU memory footprint mainly consists of four components:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>: the mini-batch data after sampling, which is closely related to batch size and fanout settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|M|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|M|</annotation></semantics></math>: the GNN model parameters and intermediate results generated during forward and backward propagation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Runtime" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Runtime</annotation></semantics></math>: the fixed memory footprint associated with the training stream context on GPUs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>: the feature cache volume.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To accommodate a broader range of scenarios, we further incorporate an intermediate parallelism mode named parallel mode <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, between the sequential mode and mode <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Profiling based analysis reveals that resource contention arising from concurrent read/write operations is primarily concentrated within the batch generation stage.
Serializing this stage significantly reduces potential memory footprint, albeit at the cost of throughput drop.
The expected throughput and GPU memory footprint of mode <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can be formulated as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title='In III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title='In III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}=f_{4}(\max\{\frac{1}{n}\mathcal{T}_{sample},\mathcal{T}_{batch}+\mathcal{T}_{train}\}\cdot iter,d(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}=f_{4}(\max\{\frac{1}{n}\mathcal{T}_{sample},\mathcal{T}_{batch}+\mathcal{T}_{train}\}\cdot iter,d(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}=f_{5}({num}_{GPU}\cdot\Theta,B,|M|,n\cdot Runtime,d(G))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}=f_{5}({num}_{GPU}\cdot\Theta,B,|M|,n\cdot Runtime,d(G)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Mode 2 mitigates the resource contention associated with concurrent access while simultaneously accelerating sampling through parallel execution.
This mode yields a notable improvement in training throughput and maintains control over memory footprint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, we design a multi-level parallelism scheduling method that addresses the limitations of existing GNN training approaches.
Current methods rely on fixed parallelism schedules, limiting their adaptability across diverse workload scenarios and hardware configurations.
By analyzing the task scenarios and performance requirements, <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN can tailor the parallel training strategy to address bottlenecks at different stages, which enables flexible trade-offs between GPU memory footprint and throughput.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Hardware Oriented Auto-Tuning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches often achieve satisfactory performance through manual tuning or default configurations, owing to their relatively simple design with fixed optimization strategies and limited reconfigurable parameters.
However, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN introduces a more sophisticated framework that incorporates diverse sampling techniques and dynamic training scheduling strategies.
This expanded functionality results in an exponentially larger configuration space, rendering traditional manual tuning methods infeasible.
Thus, efficiently generating training configurations tailored to specific scenarios and task requirements becomes critical for further performance enhancement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enable task-hardware oriented auto-tuning, we propose a three-level mechanism comprising:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Task-aware performance metrics prioritization,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hardware-aware constraints analysis,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-objective Pareto frontier exploration.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First, our <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task-aware performance metrics prioritization</span> module dynamically adjusts evaluation criteria based on task-specific requirements.
As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title='Fig. 5 ‣ III-C Task-Hardware Oriented Auto-Tuning ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we initialize the priority vector <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> with differentiated weights for various metrics (e.g., accuracy, throughput) according to their relative importance in the target training scenario.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2511.07421v1/Autotuning.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:357/308;" width="357" height="308"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Task-hardware oriented auto-tuning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For hardware considerations, the <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hardware-aware constraints analysis</span> module formally maps physical resource constraints into optimization boundaries.
For instance, given a GPU memory capacity of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> GB, we enforce strict inequality constraints (e.g., peak memory consumption <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;10</annotation></semantics></math> GB) during design space exploration (DSE).
When exploration approaches these boundaries, the weighted reward function will return extremely negative reward values <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to steer the search away from invalid configurations while maintaining optimization progress.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The core challenge then becomes efficiently predicting GNN training performance.
We extract the key configurations from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title='In III-A Locality-Aware Graph Sampling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S3.E5" title='In III-B Multi-Level Parallelism Scheduling ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and leverage existing open-source datasets and tasks to collect performance statistics for training a surrogate model.
This surrogate model takes training configurations and graph characteristics as inputs and, by integrating sophisticated machine learning techniques such as XGBoost, Regression, and Decision Trees, generates accurate performance predictions that guide the automated design space exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, our <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multi-objective Pareto frontier exploration</span> employs the reinforcement learning-based DSE algorithm, which is detailed in Algo. <a href="#alg3" title='Algorithm 3 ‣ III-C Task-Hardware Oriented Auto-Tuning ‣ III The "A"^"3"GNN Framework ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We formulate the auto-tuning task as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) and employ the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) as reinforcement learning agent.
The state, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}=\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}=\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, encompasses both the configuration settings and the corresponding predicted performance.
Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a specific configuration setting within the design space, and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=[thr,mem,acc]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=[thr,mem,acc]</annotation></semantics></math> describes the performance metrics: throughput, memory footprint, and accuracy.
The weighted reward formula (Line 10: <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{m}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{m}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>) inherently incorporates both task preferences and hardware constraints, enabling simultaneous optimization of competing objectives.
This formulation allows the discovery of Pareto-optimal configurations that optimally balance performance metrics within the feasible hardware constraints.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> PPO for <math id="alg3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN Auto-Tuning</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.7" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l0.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Metrics priority vector </span><math id="alg3.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l0.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, initial configurations </span><math id="alg3.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐩</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l0.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l0a.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Recommend configurations </span><math id="alg3.l0a.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{p}*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}*</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg3.l0a.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg3.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">while</span><span id="alg3.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> not converged </span><span id="alg3.l1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg3.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">


</span>
