<td id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.8361</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.7921</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.7303</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">Memory Footprint</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.8531</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.8841</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">0.7264</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> scores</span> indicate better precision of estimators when they are closer to <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiment ‣ Towards Affordable, Adaptive and Automatic GNN Training on CPU-GPU Heterogeneous Platforms" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, our surrogate model can make relative accurate prediction.
Furthermore, the PPO agent can nearly achieve optimal performance, and the exploration is on average <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.1\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than grid search exploration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN, a novel GNN training framework designed for Affordable, Adaptive, and Automatic GNN training.
Leveraging locality-aware sampling, <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN attains high cache hit rates while minimizing resource overhead.
This optimization yields substantial improvements in both memory footprint and throughput, making efficient GNN training accessible even with less powerful devices.
The proposed multi-level parallelism scheduling mechanism enables adaptive selection of appropriate parallel strategies based on scenario characteristics, leading to significant improvements in accuracy, memory footprint, and throughput, respectively.
To further enhance performance, <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN employs automated tuning to determine optimal configuration settings that satisfy hardware constraints while maximizing resource utilization.
Comprehensive experiments on diverse tasks and platforms show that <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN delivers <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.88\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.88</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.88\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup in GNN training with minimal accuracy degradation and only <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> additional memory consumption compared to state-of-the-art methods.
With flexible performance trade-offs, <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>A</mtext><mtext><sub class="ltx_sub"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></sub></mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{A}^{\textsubscript{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>GNN can further achieve up to <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.95\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.95</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.95\times</annotation></semantics></math> acceleration or reduce GPU memory usage to just <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> of baseline approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
